# Nicegram Build cache
# Fork-owned bazel policy. Tracked, and the single place our flags live.
#
# Loaded by every bazel invocation through one `try-import` appended to the
# upstream .bazelrc: the CLI flows, the Xcode build (rules_xcodeproj passes
# --bazelrc=.bazelrc), and a hand-run `bazel` all read it. Keeping our flags
# here instead of inline shrinks the fork's .bazelrc diff from a 24-line block
# in the middle of that file to five appended lines at its very end.
#
# try-import, not import: this file is fork-owned and always tracked, so the
# two behave identically here and the `try-import` is a statement of intent
# rather than a graceful degradation. It could not degrade gracefully anyway:
# Make.py's `common_debug_args` passes `--config=ng_dev` for every debug build
# and for project generation, and `ng_dev` is defined only here, so losing this
# file fails immediately with
# `Config value 'ng_dev' is not defined in any .rc file`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Disk cache bounds
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The disk cache had no bound and had reached 42 GB. Bazel collects it itself;
# both defaults are 0, meaning never. Values verified to parse with
# `bazel canonicalize-flags` on our vendored 8.4.2 and again on 9.2.0
# (2026-10-12: same names, still experimental, same defaults, and a bogus
# `100Q` is rejected) -- re-verify if you change the syntax or bump bazel. 9.x
# computes the main repository mapping even for canonicalize-flags, so run it
# in an empty workspace (a blank MODULE.bazel) unless build-input/ is resolved.
#
# Collection runs in the *idle* bazel server after
# --experimental_disk_cache_gc_idle_delay (default 5m), so it happens between
# builds, never during one. A machine that never leaves a server idle will not
# collect; `ci/clean-caches.sh` reports the size so that is visible.
#
# These are `build` lines, which bazel also applies to test/run/coverage.
build --experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_size=100G
build --experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_age=45d

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ng_dev: makes upstream's dead emit_swiftsourceinfo line real
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Requested by every dev flow through a single --config=ng_dev added to
# Make.py's common_debug_args, which is the one list included by both
# invoke_build() and get_project_generation_arguments(). So it reaches the
# generated xcodeproj.bazelrc -- and therefore both the Xcode build and
# ci/verify-build.sh, which since 2026-08-28 builds through rules_xcodeproj's
# command-line API and so reads that same file -- and the release lanes, which
# go through invoke_build() and get common_release_args instead.
#
# This config used to also carry eleven flags (including
# --//Telegram:disableStripping) whose only purpose was making the CLI and
# Xcode flows request byte-identical bazel configurations, so their ~1000
# swift_library compiles would produce identical action keys and share
# disk-cache entries. That sharing was measured impossible (Task 14) and the
# flags were removed. The alignment itself worked: the swiftc command lines
# for submodules/Display became character-for-character identical between
# the two flows. Sharing still did not follow, because output paths are part
# of every action key, and the two flows' configuration directory names
# differ by their `-ST-<hash>` suffix regardless of the flags above --
# measured
#   CLI   ios_arm64-dbg-ios-arm64-min15.0-ST-a7beea3c269e
#   Xcode ios_arm64-dbg-ios-arm64-min15.0-ST-9cbb67e27a8f
# with zero overlap across all seven min-version configs, and both hashes
# byte-identical to their pre-alignment values -- adding the eleven flags
# changed neither. The `-ST-` suffix derives from the Starlark transition
# path, not from flags: the Xcode flow reaches its configuration through the
# rules_xcodeproj generator label.
#
# That gap has since been closed, but not from here: ci/verify-build.sh now
# runs bazel THROUGH that generator label using rules_xcodeproj's command-line
# API, which puts it on the same transition path and so on the same action
# keys. Read that script's header for the measurements. What stands here is the
# narrower fact: no flag in this file, or in any bazelrc, can make a plain
# `bazel build <label>` share with Xcode. So do not re-add
# --//Telegram:disableStripping or the other ten flags expecting a label build
# to start sharing; it cannot, and disableStripping in particular is a flag we
# would rather not have anywhere near a build that does not need it.
#
# What's left has nothing to do with sharing: it makes upstream's intent
# real, since .bazelrc's `build:dbg --features=swift.emit_swiftsourceinfo`
# has never applied to anything -- nothing passes `--config=dbg` (`-c dbg` is
# --compilation_mode, a different flag). Upstream owns that line, so it stays
# where it is; this is where the intent actually takes effect. Release builds
# never pass --config=ng_dev and are unaffected.
build:ng_dev --features=swift.emit_swiftsourceinfo
